# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PUBLIC Firebase web config — committed on purpose.
#
# ⚠️  NEVER put a secret in this file. It is committed to a public repository.
#     Secrets belong in .env.local (gitignored) and in the Vercel dashboard.
#     The service account key in particular must never appear here.
#
# Why these are committed: Firebase client config is public by design. The
# apiKey identifies the project, it does not grant access — anyone can read
# these values out of the browser bundle of any Firebase app. Access control
# comes from Firebase Auth plus the Firestore/Storage rules, which in this
# project deny all direct client access anyway.
#
# Committing them means a build has the config everywhere — local, CI, Vercel
# preview and production — without six dashboard fields to set and mistype.
# Without this, the browser throws `auth/invalid-api-key` at sign-in.
#
# Next.js still lets real environment variables win: anything set in the Vercel
# dashboard overrides these, so pointing a deployment at a different Firebase
# project needs no code change.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=AIzaSyApCxkJHd0BokZGp3lVy9SwA-cxY8CmfDM
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=invoicebro-e0c83.firebaseapp.com
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=invoicebro-e0c83
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=invoicebro-e0c83.firebasestorage.app
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=861556037681
NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=1:861556037681:web:8c8cc6367abfd437cd1d10
